## Why This Is Asked
This question probes multi-tenant isolation, CDC-style ingestion from on-prem, cross-region DR, and governance in a single Azure stack. It tests practical design decisions beyond generic patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Delta Lake schema evolution and drift tolerance
- Delta Live Tables and incremental loads
- Purview lineage and tagging
- ADLS Gen2 cross-region replication
- Tenant ACLs and per-tenant partitioning

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: enforce per-tenant schema drift tolerance during ingest
function alignSchema(incoming, targetSchema){
  const allowed = targetSchema.fields.map(f=>f.name);
  const filtered = incoming.columns.filter(c => allowed.includes(c.name));
  return ensureTypes(filtered, targetSchema);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate cross-region failover and data consistency? 
- How would you backfill for tenants with late-arriving changes while preserving idempotence?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical design of data contracts, lineage, and governance in a multi-domain Azure data mesh, with real ingestion patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Data contracts across domains
- End-to-end lineage with Purview
- Delta Lake schema evolution and time travel
- Domain isolation via access controls
- Canary tests for contract changes

## Code Example
```javascript
// pseudo contract test for incoming record
function validateContract(record, contract) {
  // contract = {schema: {...}, required: [...], checks: [...]}
  if (!contract.required.every(k => k in record)) return false;
  // simple type checks
  for (let key of Object.keys(contract.schema)) {
    if (typeof record[key] !== contract.schema[key]) return false;
  }
  // custom checks
  return contract.checks.every(fn => fn(record));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you version contracts and rollback a breaking change?
- How would you monitor contract violations and auto-generate lineage dashboards?
